10) 1ST OPTION TO HANDLE CONFLICT — STORE SIBLINGS
===============================================================================

Ab main gold concept.

Agar system decide nahi kar pa raha kaunsi write correct hai,
toh overwrite nahi karega.

Instead:
BOTH versions store karega.

This is called:
STORE SIBLINGS

Used in:
- Riak
- Dynamo style DBs
- some multi-value KV stores

Format:
key = counter
values = [
   {value:1, VV:{D:1,B:0}},
   {value:1, VV:{D:0,B:1}}
]

Ye siblings hain.

Meaning:
system bol raha hai:
"mere paas 2 valid concurrent truths hain"

===============================================================================
11) REAL LIFE EXAMPLE — SHOPPING CART
===============================================================================

Best example for HLD interview.

Cart initial:
{}

Phone pe user adds:
{milk}
VV {phone:1}

Laptop pe same time:
{bread}
VV {laptop:1}

Concurrent.

Agar overwrite kar diya:
milk ya bread lose ho jayega.

Siblings store:
[
 {milk},
 {bread}
]

App merge:
{milk, bread}

This is PERFECT business resolution.

Interview killer line:
For user-generated set-like data, sibling storage preserves intent and
enables semantic merges.

===============================================================================
12) DISTRIBUTED COUNTER + SIBLINGS
===============================================================================

Counter ke case mein siblings aur bhi useful.

Replica A:
1  VV {A:1,B:0}

Replica B:
1  VV {A:0,B:1}

Store siblings:
[1,1]

Application merge:
SUM = 2

Now final resolved:
2
VV merge = {A:1,B:1}

This is exactly why simple LWW is BAD for counters.

